WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:29.400
In the 1980s, in the communist Eastern Germany, if you owned a typewriter,

00:29.400 --> 00:38.400
you had to register it with the government. You had to register a sample sheet of text out of the typewriter,

00:38.400 --> 00:43.400
and this was done so the government could track where text was coming from.

00:43.400 --> 00:53.400
If they found a paper which had wrong kinds of thoughts, they could track down who created that thought.

00:53.400 --> 01:02.400
And we in the West couldn't understand how anybody would do this, how much this would restrict freedom of speech.

01:02.400 --> 01:07.400
We would never do that in our own countries.

01:07.400 --> 01:19.400
But today, in 2011, if you go and buy a color laser printer from any major laser printer manufacturer and print a page,

01:19.400 --> 01:32.400
that page will end up having slight yellow dots printed on every single page in a pattern which makes the page unique to you and to your printer.

01:32.400 --> 01:42.400
This is happening to us today. And nobody seems to be making a fuss about it.

01:43.400 --> 01:56.400
And this is an example on the ways that our own governments are using technology against us, the citizens.

01:56.400 --> 02:01.400
And this is one of the main three sources of online problems today.

02:01.400 --> 02:07.400
If we look at what's really happening in the online world, we can group the attacks based on the attackers.

02:07.400 --> 02:09.400
We have three main groups.

02:09.400 --> 02:15.400
We have online criminals, like here we have Mr. Dmitry Kolubov from the city of Kiev in Ukraine.

02:15.400 --> 02:20.400
And the motives of online criminals are very easy to understand.

02:20.400 --> 02:22.400
These guys make money.

02:22.400 --> 02:28.400
They use online attacks to make lots of money, and lots and lots of it.

02:28.400 --> 02:35.400
We actually have several cases of millionaires online, multi-millionaires who made money with their attacks.

02:35.400 --> 02:38.400
Here's Vladimir Cacin from Tartu in Estonia.

02:38.400 --> 02:44.400
This is Alfred Gonzalez. This is Steven Watt. This is Björn Sundin.

02:44.400 --> 02:49.400
This is Matthew Anderson, Tariq Aldeur, and so on and so on.

02:49.400 --> 03:02.400
These guys make their fortunes online, but they make it through the illegal means of using things like banking trojans to steal money from our bank accounts while we do online banking.

03:02.400 --> 03:10.400
Or with keyloggers to collect our credit card information while we are doing online shopping from an infected computer.

03:10.400 --> 03:18.400
The US Secret Service two months ago froze the Swiss bank account of Mr. Sam Jain right here.

03:18.400 --> 03:23.400
And that bank account had 14.9 million US dollars on it when it was frozen.

03:23.400 --> 03:27.400
Mr. Jain himself is on the loose. Nobody knows where he is.

03:28.400 --> 03:41.400
And I claim it's already today that it's more likely for any of us to become a victim of a crime online than here in the real world.

03:41.400 --> 03:49.400
And it's very obvious that this is only going to get worse. In the future, the majority of crime will be happening online.

03:50.400 --> 03:58.400
The second major group of attackers that we are watching today are not motivated by money. They're motivated by something else.

03:58.400 --> 04:04.400
Motivated by protest, motivated by an opinion, motivated by the laughs.

04:04.400 --> 04:14.400
Groups like Anonymous have risen up over the last 12 months and have become a major player in the field of online attacks.

04:15.400 --> 04:19.400
So those are the three main attackers. Criminals who do it for the money.

04:19.400 --> 04:23.400
Hacktivists like Anonymous doing it for the protest.

04:23.400 --> 04:29.400
But then the last group are nation states. Governments doing the attacks.

04:31.400 --> 04:35.400
And then we look at cases like what happened in Diginotar.

04:35.400 --> 04:41.400
This is a prime example of what happens when governments attacks against their own citizens.

04:42.400 --> 04:48.400
Diginotar is a certificate authority from the Netherlands. Or actually it was.

04:48.400 --> 04:55.400
It was running the bankruptcy last fall because they were hacked into.

04:55.400 --> 04:59.400
Somebody broke into and they hacked it thoroughly.

04:59.400 --> 05:06.400
And I asked last week in a meeting with Dutch government representatives.

05:07.400 --> 05:19.400
I asked one of the leaders of the team whether he found plausible that people died because of the Diginotar hack.

05:21.400 --> 05:24.400
And his answer was yes.

05:25.400 --> 05:30.400
So how do people die as a result of a hack like this?

05:30.400 --> 05:36.400
Well Diginotar is a CA. They sell certificates. What do you do with certificates?

05:36.400 --> 05:42.400
Well you need a certificate if you have a website that has HTTPS, SSL encrypted services.

05:42.400 --> 05:46.400
Services like Gmail.

05:46.400 --> 05:50.400
Now we all, a big part of us, uses Gmail as one of their competitors.

05:50.400 --> 05:56.400
But these services are especially popular in totalitarian states like Iran.

05:56.400 --> 06:01.400
Where dissidents use foreign services like Gmail.

06:01.400 --> 06:07.400
Because they know they are more trustworthy than the local services and they are encrypted over SSL connections.

06:07.400 --> 06:11.400
So the local government can snoop on their discussions.

06:11.400 --> 06:16.400
Except they can if they hack into a foreign CA and issue rogue certificates.

06:16.400 --> 06:21.400
And this is exactly what happened with the case of Diginotar.

06:22.400 --> 06:29.400
What about Arab Spring and things that have been happening for example in Egypt?

06:29.400 --> 06:37.400
Well in Egypt the rioters looted the headquarters of the Egypt secret police in April 2011.

06:37.400 --> 06:40.400
And when they were looting the building they found lots of papers.

06:40.400 --> 06:44.400
Among those papers this binder titled Fin Fisher.

06:44.400 --> 06:49.400
And within that binder were notes from a company based in Germany.

06:49.400 --> 07:00.400
Which had sold the Egypt government a set of tools for intercepting and in very large scale all the communication of the citizens of the country.

07:00.400 --> 07:05.400
They had sold this tool for 280,000 euros to the Egypt government.

07:05.400 --> 07:08.400
The company headquarters are right here.

07:08.400 --> 07:15.400
So western governments are providing totalitarian governments with tools to do this against their own citizens.

07:15.400 --> 07:19.400
But western governments are doing it themselves as well.

07:19.400 --> 07:26.400
For example in Germany just a couple of weeks ago the so called Stadts Trojan was found.

07:26.400 --> 07:32.400
Which was a Trojan used by German government officials to investigate their own citizens.

07:32.400 --> 07:38.400
If you are a suspect in a criminal case, well it's pretty obvious your phone will be tapped.

07:38.400 --> 07:41.400
But today it goes beyond that.

07:41.400 --> 07:48.400
They will tap your internet connection, they will even use tools like Stadts Trojan to infect your computer with a Trojan.

07:48.400 --> 07:57.400
Which enables them to watch all your communication, to listen to your online discussions, to collect your passwords.

07:57.400 --> 08:14.400
And when we think deeper about things like this, the obvious response from people should be that okay well that sounds bad.

08:14.400 --> 08:18.400
But that doesn't really affect me because I'm a legal citizen.

08:18.400 --> 08:21.400
Why should I worry because I have nothing to hide.

08:21.400 --> 08:25.400
And this is an argument which doesn't make sense.

08:26.400 --> 08:33.400
Privacy is implied. Privacy is not up for discussion.

08:33.400 --> 08:42.400
This is not a question between privacy against security.

08:42.400 --> 08:48.400
It's a question of freedom against control.

08:49.400 --> 08:59.400
And while we might trust our governments right now, right here in 2011, any rights we give away will be given away for good.

08:59.400 --> 09:07.400
And do we trust, do we blindly trust any future government, a government we might have 50 years from now?

09:11.400 --> 09:16.400
And these are the questions that we have to worry about for the next 50 years.

09:18.400 --> 09:22.400
Thank you for watching.

